s also tells  us much about  their author's respect of cultural and  social worlds  although in differing ways  one repeated criticism by outsiders has  been that  unlike that of the west indian history  writing does not have any real  biographies  but the ramayana is the life story of  rama  in enoch terms rama is the god visnu  born on earth but he's still born as a  human  with human limitations and the life  story  further one main purpose of rama's birth  and of the ramayana  itself is to teach moral behavior dharma  to people many western biographers  also use their subject's life to teach  moral lessons  and many people read them in order to  find role models to emulate or  avoid so in this sense the life story of  rama  compares to the genre of biography  but since the rama of the ramayana was  in etic terms not a real person  many outsiders might not consider this  text a real  biography but rather the amalgamation of  some possible historical facts about one  or more princes  compiled over a long period of time  this text also reflects the larger  historical processes  of sanskrit-speaking north indians  spreading their influence  across the dravidian language speaking  south  the setting of the ramayana includes  some very real places  and social formations recall that from  about the sixth century bce onward  especially in the north indian gangetic  plain hundreds of locally based  janapadas or  people's places were developing into  small kingdoms  one of these kingdoms was gosala with  its capital at ayodhya  where the ramayana begins and ends  most of these kingdoms had a raja a king  who fought  or allied with his neighbors to expand  or just to survive  gradually kshatriya martial dharma or  code  for moral conduct became the model for  many of these competing  and collaborating kings and princes  first in north india  and then in parts of central and south  india as well  in most north indian hindu kingdoms some  brahmanas serve this royalty as advisors  priests teachers genealogists or bards  the ramayana may have begun as a bardic  epic  by a courtier about a noble prince of  ayodhya  beneath brahmanas and kashatrias and  ideally under their protection  were merchants peasants artisans and  other subjects  but they do not appear very often in the  ramayana except as attendance on the  royal court  or as subjects seeking redress of  grievances from the king  or as foot soldiers in their armies  so ramayana is mainly an elite text  primarily focused on rajas their  extended families  their supporters servants and enemies  both demonic  and human now emek and etic ideas about  the complex  authorship of the ramayana differ in  significant ways  based on different ways of knowing  history  a leading emec view credits the sage  valmiki  with composing the most authoritative  original  sanskrit telling of the ramayana  but the emec view also presents the  author differently  from how etic historians might  understand the role  either of an historian or a creative  writer  of literature in valmiki's own  telling he himself only participates as  a relatively  minor figure in the actual events of the  ramayana  valmiki was a reformed bandit who had  achieved great merit through becoming a  forest-dwelling ascetic  it is in his rustic hermitage where the  main figures  rama and his wife sita rest early in  their travels  toward the end of the ramayana sita  returns to take refuge in valmiki's  hermitage to give birth  and raise rama's and her twin sons  so as an eyewitness valmiki personally  participates  relatively little in the events  instead as the ramayana explains valmiki  hears  rama's whole story from the demigod  narada  and then is ordered by the creator god  brahma to retell it  valmiki also meditates deeply  and gains cosmic insights about what has  happened  learning things that he had not known  before  so in the emet tradition valmiki bases  his account on divine information and  guidance  not primarily on his being an eyewitness  finally valmiki teaches the ramayana to  rama's own sons who recite it for the  first time in public  to rama himself who would have corrected  any errors  if there had been any therefore in its  own emic terms  the truth of this story can't be doubted  but what did valmiki actually personally  create  here the emek tradition credits valmiki  with inventing the specific  32-syllable poetic meter called ashloka  in which the ramayana and various other  classical  sanskrit poems were recited but how did  valmiki come up with a shloka meter  in the ramayana valmiki himself explains  that he was observing  two birds in loving union just then a  forest dwelling human hunter perhaps  anadavasi  shot the male bird dead leaving the  female bird in mortal agony  valmiki spontaneously cursed the hunter  to wander eternally this curse was in  the shloka meter  and that was valmiki's original  contribution making him  the adikavi or first poet  many later authors composed their  sanskrit poetry  using the same shloka meter  but in edict terms valmiki could not  have been the sole author of the  ramayana  given its sheer volume and its many  centuries of composition  in the current form of the sanskrit  ramayana attributed to valmiki  the ramayana is 50 thousand lines long  which is double homer's odyssey and  virgilson need combined  further linguists and historians of  religion analyze the text as a product  of about 900 years  of adding to altering and editing by  many sequential authors  each of whom however gave valmiki credit  for the entire text  now let's turn to the emic life history  of rama as presented  in valmiki's ramayana given the great  length of this text we'll have to  concentrate  on the account of rama's life the main  subject and leave out  many of the vast numbers of episodes and  the myriad of branch stories interwoven  with it  king dasharata rules the north indian  kingdom of yoja  while both eric and emec scholars agree  that this was a real place  the center for an expanse of janapada  there's no  ethic evidence of this particular king  nonetheless although the emacly real  king dasharata rules virtuously  he has no male heirs so he hires brahman  priests to perform a great sacrifice  earning him much merit indeed out of the  sacrificial fire arises a vessel  filled with a special rice milk  each of his three queens consumes some  of it and each becomes pregnant with  sons  the middle queen takes two portions so  she has twin sons  while these events are taking place on  earth in heaven the hindu gods face a  major problem  a ten-headed demon named ravana himself  performed great austerities  and an awesome sacrifice which gained  him the power to demand from the gods  that he be made invincible against any  god  or demon but in his overweening pride  ravana does not ask to be invulnerable  against a human arrogantly  ravana then oppresses brahmanas  disrupting their religious rights and  threatening  the moral order of the universe  so the great god vishnu agrees to be  born in the world as human and thus  able to kill ravana visnu  therefore voluntarily descends to earth  taking birth as dhatarata's four sons  rama and his three brothers  simultaneously but mostly as rama  the son of the elder senior queen  as valmiki describes in much detail rama  grows up as a perfect prince always  following his kshatriya dharma  there are long passages wherever amiki  recounts how rama  selflessly defeats evil forces  especially  respecting and protecting forest  dwelling ascetics  he thus explicitly and implicitly models  the ideal behavior  for not just princes but for all hindus  to follow  when rama is ready to marry he enters a  swamp for  sita the princess of a neighboring  kingdom swayamvara  literally means that a woman chooses her  own husband  but the ramayana describes this as a  contest among suitors  whichever man is able to string a  massive bow  wins the bride only rama's great merit  enables him to win the contest  how then is this the wedding of the  bride's own  choosing well in emic terms the bride's  own  karma or merit determined who would win  in emec terms sita is a goddess lakshmi  that is  visnu's wife voluntarily born on earth  in order  to be rama's bride so this is the  inevitable outcome  of the contest in this  and many other episodes the ramayana  shows exactly how the system of karma  works  everything that happens is the  appropriate result  of the meritorious or de-meritorious  actions  of everyone involved in that action  while the details are in edit terms  superhuman  the evidence about bridal and other  contests among princes  and marriage alliances among kingdoms  fits with our other historical evidence  about that time period  eventually king dasarata decides to  abdicate and crown rama  as the new king but as rama's coronation  approaches  his youngest stepmother kaikai becomes  jealous and schemes to have her own son  bharata  made the next king by her meritorious  action  earlier in her life kaikai has earned  two promises owed to her  by king dasarata she uses the first boon  to get the reluctant dasarata to declare  bharata as his heir  and the second to send rama into exile  into the forest for 14 years  dasaratha then dies cursing kaiki for he  for her improper and selfish desire so  she herself gains no advantage  from her improper request  this moment of crisis enables valmiki  and the other authors of the sanskrit  ramayana  to illustrate further moral lessons  rama virtuously accepts his father's  order  however reluctantly given and that goes  that very day willingly into exile  his wife sita voluntarily goes with him  taking on the hardships of forest life  and thus showing how in enoch terms  an ideal wife should act  for his part the brother bharata refuses  to accept the throne  that his mother improperly obtained for  him  instead he governs in rama's name taking  on all the burdens of rule  with none of the benefits barta is after  all  vishnu on earth as well so he  illustrates how a younger brother should  act  respectfully toward his elder brother  one of their middle twin brothers  lakshmina  also goes with rama to serve him in the  forest while the other  twin chatrugana stays with bharata to  assist him  much of the lengthy ramayana recounts  the trials and virtues  of rama and sita in the forest as they  travel from ayodhya  south across india this is where the  epic describes great diversity of the  geography  and the societies of india throughout  rama exemplifies the proper dharmic  behavior  in a number of trying situations in one  a lustful sister of the demon king  ravana  named surprinka tries to seduce both  rama and his brother lakshmana  when rama declines her offer explaining  that he's already married to sita  sir peraka offers to kill her and take  sita's place  finally lakshmana disgraces surprise for  her  improper lust the pivotal point in the  ramayana comes when the demon king  ravana determines to carry off sita  ravana does this out of his lascivious  lust for her  and also to get revenge for his  mutilated sister  naka so the demons generally all act  with  excessive desire well that is their  dharma  they also receive punishments for acting  on it  in order to carry off sita ravana has  one of his demon courtiers take on the  form of a golden deer  who prances before sita and entices her  to desire it as a pet  for this sita herself begins to  accumulate  demerits at least according to the  prevailing emic interpretation  that make her vulnerable to being  carried off by ravana  sita urges rama to chase after the  golden deer  rama sets off after it as it keepers  just out of range  but rama does not accumulate demerit for  being deceived  by the disguised demon saying if it's a  deer there's no harm in hunting it  but if it's a demon then it's a dharma  to kill it anyway  he shoots and mortally wounds the dear  disguised  demon but in his dying breath  this demon cries out in rama's voice for  lakshmana's help  hearing this sita is again deceived into  doubting rama's prowess  and urges lakshmina to leave her and go  help rama  lakshmina refuses to leave sita  unguarded  but then she unjustly criticizes him and  lakshmana is thus forced  to go when ravana approaches  in the disguise of a brahmana mendican  sita is again deceived  about how to act in accordance with her  dharma she moves forward  to offer him food and thereby comes  under  ravana's power one emic lesson here  is that women even perfect women like  sita are susceptible to desire  and misperception of the true nature of  situations  but a seat is carried off all her  demerits are burned off as well  and thereafter in the ramayana sita  always acts in accordance with her  dharma  another result of this abduction is that  ravana's death is now assured  rama has the dharmic justification to  kill him  which was the prime purpose of vishnu  taking birth as rama in the  on earth in the first place  in order to perform his dharma by  recovering sita  rama recruits the local population of  monkeys and bears as his allies  most notable among these is hanuman a  divinely descended monkey who becomes  rama's leading servant  and a very popular deity in his own  right  hanuman is known for his strength and so  is worshipped by many wrestlers today  he's also the most faithful servant and  so an emec role model  for lower-born people rama's local  allies search  everywhere to no avail finally haneman  leaps across the water talanka  the kingdom of ravana today most hindus  believe that hanuman  crossed the narrow strait now called the  gulf of manor  between india and sri lanka island  there hanuman finds sita confined but  resistant  to ravana's enticements hanuman offers  to rescue her himself  but she insists that only rama should  liberate her  since that is his dharma as her husband  hanuman returns to rama with the news  but to invade  lanka rama and his allies have to build  a bridge  this bridge provides a good example of  how etic and emic evidence  coincide and yet also differ there is  indeed a shallow  underwater limestone ridge over the 20  miles between south india  and sri lanka this ridge is clearly  visible from satellite photographs  for edec geologists the underwater ridge  has natural causes  and needs no sacred source  in emec terms however this bridge was  constructed by hanuman  and as allied bears and monkeys using  the power of rama's name  to make it secure although later storms  sank part of his bridge  underwater for many devotees of rama  these satellite photographs are  scientific  evidence for the truth of the ramayana  although that sacred text  needs no external proof  in the great war between rama's forces  and ravana's demon armies  many battles and perils are detailed  each with a moral lesson attached  for instance at one point lakshmana is  gravely wounded  and about to be carried off by the  exultant demons  but lakshmi remembers that he is vishnu  incarnate and therefore  invulnerable at the same time however  lakshna must be human or he cannot aid  in the killing of ravana  to revive lakshmina hanaman is sent off  to a distant mountain to bring back a  special  healing herb on his arrival there he  realizes he isn't educated enough to  identify the needed  medicinal plant so he cleverly brings  back the entire mountain  enabling the physicians at lakshman  aside to select the herb and cure him  this emily shows how even uneducated  servants  can cleverly use their brute strength in  devoted service  to their elite masters  finally rama kills ravana and liberates  sita  when she rushes to rejoin rama however  he repudiates her as having been  impure by living under ravana's power  for some emek commentators this shows  that rama only acted out of moral duty  not his personal desire for zita  other analysts in india and the west  critique rama for blaming the 