uage for example cumben  in the early 13th century retold the  story in tamil  a prominent dravidian language of  southeast india  today there are over 75 million speakers  of tamil  more than the number of people who speak  italian for example  etamil is only one of india's many  regional languages  each of these later authors using his or  her own language  highlights different features of rama's  story  removing episodes present in valmiki's  telling  adding others and thus changing the  epic's length  content and purpose the 16th century  mughal emperor akbar whom we'll discuss  in a later  lecture had his muslim officials  supervise the translation of the  ramayana  into the persian language of his  imperial court as a major work of both  history  and also moral teaching  many devotional films for example in the  popular bollywood and various regional  cinema styles have used parts of the  ramayana  as their plot in recent years various  television producers  have used that medium to present the  story of rama  the most famous of these hindi language  television programs  were produced and directed by ramanand  sagar and his production company  he based his telling both on valmiki and  also on the 16th century tulsidas  the most famous of the retellers of  rama's story in hindi  initially there were 78 episodes  broadcast one  each sunday morning in 1987 88  on the government's official network  soccer's team then repeatedly remade  these  for broadcast on other networks over the  next 30 years  many spin-offs sequels and other  versions  have also been broadcast  there have also been many translations  and retellings in english  most recently from valmiki sanskrit  telling  under the general editorship of american  scholars  robert and sally goldman but critics  of the brahmanic system of arnaz and  dharma have also used the story of rama  to prove a very different history for  example  there is a substantial jati or birth  group that proudly calls itself the  descendants  and followers of valmiki although elite  hindus used to class them as untouchable  before untouchability became illegal in  today's india  so various communities have challenged  their low status  in the brahmonic system by using the  prestige of  thoraminum  over the last few centuries many fiction  writers have also used  episodes or characters from the ramayana  to convey a variety of messages  that clash with those of valmiki's  tellings  feminist short story authors and  playwrights celebrate sita  often portraying her as a strong  independent woman  wronged by an insensitive husband  some social reformers have critiqued  valmiki's  and tulsidas's orthodox tellings of the  life of rama  claiming those elite authors justify his  abuses  of his lower class and lower varna  subjects  most prominently a culturalist  nationalist movement  in the tamil speaking region of south  india called the dravidian organization  or dk movement  started in the 1930s to attack brahman  domination  although brahmanas were a relatively  small minority  in the tamil region they controlled much  of the political  and economic power there under the  charismatic  eevee ramasamy known as evr  this dravidian movement pronounced the  ramayana to be the history  of an unjust invasion by north indians  according to evr valmiki describes the  inhabitants of south india as merely  monkeys and bears  and the inhabitants of sri lanka as  demons  evr argued in print and dramatized in  the streets of that  tamil speakers and other southerners  must discredit  the brahmanic religious system that  treated them as  sub-human until they did that they would  remain  mentally under the north indian  brahmanic cultural hegemony or  domination  therefore evr mounted massive public  processions  in which images of rama were beaten with  defiling shoes  and pictures of him burnt to show the  contempt the tamils  should have for rama and brahmanism  out of this tamil cultural movement came  the political parties that have  dominated the government  in tamilnadu state for many decades  the narrative and sacred power of the  ramayana has meant that it spread beyond  india  to southeast asia especially in  indonesia cambodia  thailand and burma now myanmar  in indonesia an account of rama in old  javanese dates from about 870.  one of java's most popular shadow puppet  plays  also features the story of rama  cambodia's famous 12th century angkor  wat temple  has many stone carved panels and statues  illustrating the ramayana then around  1350  the king of thailand renamed his capital  ayutya  after rama's capital ayoja  from the 18th century a burmese version  of the ramayana became the burmese  national epic  so we'll as we'll see for buddhism and  islam hindu cultural influences spread  from india far to the east but as rama's  story moved  local cultures adapted it to their own  cultural values and artistic forms  although their attitudes toward the  story of rama their methodologies and  their conclusions often differ  both emic and ethic readers can learn  much  about the ancient indian history from  ramayana  in the next couple of lectures we'll  discuss in similar ways the other  major indian epic the mahabharata and  its account of ancient  indian history  [Music]  you

