y on his being an eyewitness  finally valmiki teaches the ramayana to  rama's own sons who recite it for the  first time in public  to rama himself who would have corrected  any errors  if there had been any therefore in its  own emic terms  the truth of this story can't be doubted  but what did valmiki actually personally  create  here the emek tradition credits valmiki  with inventing the specific  32-syllable poetic meter called ashloka  in which the ramayana and various other  classical  sanskrit poems were recited but how did  valmiki come up with a shloka meter  in the ramayana valmiki himself explains  that he was observing  two birds in loving union just then a  forest dwelling human hunter perhaps  anadavasi  shot the male bird dead leaving the  female bird in mortal agony  valmiki spontaneously cursed the hunter  to wander eternally this curse was in  the shloka meter  and that was valmiki's original  contribution making him  the adikavi or first poet  many later authors composed their  sanskrit poetry  using the same shloka meter  but in edict terms valmiki could not  have been the sole author of the  ramayana  given its sheer volume and its many  centuries of composition  in the current form of the sanskrit  ramayana attributed to valmiki  the ramayana is 50 thousand lines long  which is double homer's odyssey and  virgilson need combined  further linguists and historians of  religion analyze the text as a product  of about 900 years  of adding to altering and editing by  many sequential authors  each of whom however gave valmiki credit  for the entire text  now let's turn to the emic life history  of rama as presented  in valmiki's ramayana given the great  length of this text we'll have to  concentrate  on the account of rama's life the main  subject and leave out  many of the vast numbers of episodes and  the myriad of branch stories interwoven  with it  king dasharata rules the north indian  kingdom of yoja  while both eric and emec scholars agree  that this was a real place  the center for an expanse of janapada  there's no  ethic evidence of this particular king  nonetheless although the emacly real  king dasharata rules virtuously  he has no male heirs so he hires brahman  priests to perform a great sacrifice  earning him much merit indeed out of the  sacrificial fire arises a vessel  filled with a special rice milk  each of his three queens consumes some  of it and each becomes pregnant with  sons  the middle queen takes two portions so  she has twin sons  while these events are taking place on  earth in heaven the hindu gods face a  major problem  a ten-headed demon named ravana himself  performed great austerities  and an awesome sacrifice which gained  him the power to demand from the gods  that he be made invincible against any  god  or demon but in his overweening pride  ravana does not ask to be invulnerable  against a human arrogantly  ravana then oppresses brahmanas  disrupting their religious rights and  threatening  the moral order of the universe  so the great god vishnu agrees to be  born in the world as human and thus  able to kill ravana visnu  therefore voluntarily descends to earth  taking birth as dhatarata's four sons  rama and his three brothers  simultaneously but mostly as rama  the son of the elder senior queen  as valmiki describes in much detail rama  grows up as a perfect prince always  following his kshatriya dharma  there are long passages wherever amiki  recounts how rama  selflessly defeats evil forces  especially  respecting and protecting forest  dwelling ascetics  he thus explicitly and implicitly models  the ideal behavior  for not just princes but for all hindus  to follow  when rama is ready to marry he enters a  swamp for  sita the princess of a neighboring  kingdom swayamvara  literally means that a woman chooses her  own husband  but the ramayana describes this as a  contest among suitors  whichever man is able to string a  massive bow  wins the bride only rama's great merit  enables him to win the contest  how then is this the wedding of the  bride's own  choosing well in emic terms the bride's  own  karma or merit determined who would win  in emec terms sita is a goddess lakshmi  that is  visnu's wife voluntarily born on earth  in order  to be rama's bride so this is the  inevitable outcome  of the contest in this  and many other episodes the ramayana  shows exactly how the system of karma  works  everything that happens is the  appropriate result  of the meritorious or de-meritorious  actions  of everyone involved in that action  while the details are in edit terms  superhuman  the evidence about bridal and other  contests among princes  and marriage alliances among kingdoms  fits with our other historical evidence  about that time period  eventually king dasarata decides to  abdicate and crown rama  as the new king but as rama's coronation  approaches  his youngest stepmother kaikai becomes  jealous and schemes to have her own son  bharata  made the next king by her meritorious  action  earlier in her life kaikai has earned  two promises owed to her  by king dasarata she uses the first boon  to get the reluctant dasarata to declare  bharata as his heir  and the second to send rama into exile  into the forest for 14 years  dasaratha then dies cursing kaiki for he  for her improper and selfish desire so  she herself gains no advantage  from her improper request  this moment of crisis enables valmiki  and the other authors of the sanskrit  ramayana  to illustrate further moral lessons  rama virtuously accepts his father's  order  however reluctantly given and that goes  that very day willingly into exile  his wife sita voluntarily goes with him  taking on the hardships of forest life  and thus showing how in enoch terms  an ideal wife should act  for his part the brother bharata refuses  to accept the throne  that his mother improperly obtained for  him  instead he governs in rama's name taking  on all the burdens of rule  with none of the benefits barta is after  all  vishnu on earth as well so he  illustrates how a younger brother should  act  respectfully toward his elder brother  one of their middle twin brothers  lakshmina  also goes with rama to serve him in the  forest while the other  twin chatrugana stays with bharata to  assist him  much of the lengthy ramayana recounts  the trials and virtues  of rama and sita in the forest as they  travel from ayodhya  south across india this is where the  epic describes great diversity of the  geography  and the societies of india throughout  rama exemplifies the proper dharmic  behavior  in a number of trying situations in one  a lustful sister of the demon king  ravana  named surprinka tries to seduce both  rama and his brother lakshmana  when rama declines her offer explaining  that he's already married to sita  sir peraka offers to kill her and take  sita's place  finally lakshmana disgraces surprise for  her  improper lust the pivotal point in the  ramayana comes when the demon king  ravana determines to carry off sita  ravana does this out of his lascivious  lust for her  and also to get revenge for his  mutilated sister  naka so the demons generally all act  with  excessive desire well that is their  dharma  they also receive punishments for acting  on it  in order to carry off sita ravana has  one of his demon courtiers take on the  form of a golden deer  who prances before sita and entices her  to desire it as a pet  for this sita herself begins to  accumulate  demerits at least according to the  prevailing emic interpretation  that make her vulnerable to being  carried off by ravana  sita urges rama to chase after the  golden deer  rama sets off after it as it keepers  just out of range  but rama does not accumulate demerit for  being deceived  by the disguised demon saying if it's a  deer there's no harm in hunting it  but if it's a demon then it's a dharma  to kill it anyway  he shoots and mortally wounds the dear  disguised  demon but in his dying breath  this demon cries out in rama's voice for  lakshmana's help  hearing this sita is again deceived into  doubting rama's prowess  and urges lakshmina to leave her and go  help rama  lakshmina refuses to leave sita  unguarded  but then she unjustly criticizes him and  lakshmana is thus forced  to go when ravana approaches  in the disguise of a brahmana mendican  sita is again deceived  about how to act in accordance with her  dharma she moves forward  to offer him food and thereby comes  under  ravana's power one emic lesson here  is that women even perfect women like  sita are susceptible to desire  and misperception of the true nature of  situations  but a seat is carried off all her  demerits are burned off as well  and thereafter in the ramayana sita  always acts in accordance with her  dharma  another result of this abduction is that  ravana's death is now assured  rama has the dharmic justification to  kill him  which was the prime purpose of vishnu  taking birth as rama in the  on earth in the first place  in order to perform his dharma by  recovering sita  rama recruits the local population of  monkeys and bears as his allies  most notable among these is hanuman a  divinely descended monkey who becomes  rama's leading servant  and a very popular deity in his own  right  hanuman is known for his strength and so  is worshipped by many wrestlers today  he's also the most faithful servant and  so an emec role model  for lower-born people rama's local  allies search  everywhere to no avail finally haneman  leaps across the water talanka  the kingdom of ravana today most hindus  believe that hanuman  crossed the narrow strait now called the  gulf of manor  between india and sri lanka island  there hanuman finds sita confined but  resistant  to ravana's enticements hanuman offers  to rescue her himself  but she insists that only rama should  liberate her  since that is his dharma as her husband  hanuman returns to rama with the news  but to invade  lanka rama and his allies have to build  a bridge  this bridge provides a good example of  how etic and emic evidence  coincide and yet also differ there is  indeed a shallow  underwater limestone ridge over the 20  miles between south india  and sri lanka this ridge is clearly  visible from satellite photographs  for edec geologists the underwater ridge  has natural causes  and needs no sacred source  in emec terms however this bridge was  constructed by hanuman  and as allied bears and monkeys using  the power of rama's name  to make it secure although later storms  sank part of his bridge  underwater for many devotees of rama  these satellite photographs are  scientific  evidence for the truth of the ramayana  although that sacred text  needs no external proof  in the great war between rama's forces  and ravana's demon armies  many battles and perils are detailed  each with a moral lesson attached  for instance at one point lakshmana is  gravely wounded  and about to be carried off by the  exultant demons  but lakshmi remembers that he is vishnu  incarnate and therefore  invulnerable at the same time however  lakshna must be human or he cannot aid  in the killing of ravana  to revive lakshmina hanaman is sent off  to a distant mountain to bring back a  special  healing herb on his arrival there he  realizes he isn't educated enough to  identify the needed  medicinal plant so he cleverly brings  back the entire mountain  enabling the physicians at lakshman  aside to select the herb and cure him  this emily shows how even uneducated  servants  can cleverly use their brute strength in  devoted service  to their elite masters  finally rama kills ravana and liberates  sita  when she rushes to rejoin rama however  he repudiates her as having been  impure by living under ravana's power  for some emek commentators this shows  that rama only acted out of moral duty  not his personal desire for zita  other analysts in india and the west  critique rama for blaming the victim  sita for her unwilling abduction  just as so many abducted women have been  blamed by their family and by society  in so many cultures valmiki has sita  then  enter a sacred fire to prove her purity  to the world  only then does rama accept her back to  explain his action  rama proclaims that he knew all along  that she was pure  but since less enlightened people might  not understand that  he had to demonstrate her purity to them  by this fiery test  after the return of rama and sita to a  yoja they rule as king and queen  completely virtuously even today the  phrase  rama raja or rama's reign conveys  for many orthodox hindus a period of  perfect governance  that present-day politicians should  imitate  but once again the unenlightened  subjects of a yoja misperceive dharma  and begin to ignorantly  question sita's virtue  so rama feels he must send sita away  banishing her to valmiki's hermitage  unaware  that she is pregnant with his two sons  as use these sons come to rama's court  and recite thoramayana  which valmiki has taught them then sita  returns to her mother earth  becoming her divine form of lakshmi  again  finally rama too ends his earthly life  and again becomes the god vishnu in  edict terms  rama may have been a real prince or  several princes  whom local people deified and then  brahmanas later sanskritized  into the vedic god visnu as one of his  incarnations  in emec terms rama is a beloved deity to  whom people can relate  often more accessibly revered than a  more distant visnu  as filled as a ramayana is with moral  lessons  brand stories and numerous episodes it  is also a biography of rama  to many hindus today every word of  al-miki's telling  is absolute truth but for many others  hindus and non-hindus the story of rama  could be used for other purposes  after valmiki many other authors retold  the story of rama in slightly or greatly  different  ways each region of india has one or  more tellings of rama's story  in its own language for example cumben  in the early 13th century retold the  story in tamil  a prominent dravidian language of  southeast india  today there are over 75 million speakers  of tamil  more than the number of people who speak  italian for example  etamil is only one of india's many  regional languages  each of these later authors using his or  her own language  highlights different features of rama's  story  removing episodes present in valmiki's  telling  adding others and thus changing the  epic's length  content and purpose the 16th century  mughal emperor akbar whom we'll discuss  in a later  lecture had his muslim officials  supervise the translation of the  ramayana  into the persian language of his  imperial court as a major work of both  history  and also moral teaching  many devotional films for example in the  popular bollywood and various regional  cinema styles have used parts of the  ramayana  as their plot in recent years various  television producers  have used that medium to present the  story of rama  the most famous of these hindi language  television programs  were produced and directed by ramanand  sagar and his production company  he based his telling both on valmiki and  also on the 16th century tulsidas  the most famous of the retellers of  rama's story in hindi  initially there were 78 episodes  broadcast one  each sunday morning in 1987 88  on the government's official network  soccer's team then repeatedly remade  these  for broadcast on other networks over the  next 30 years  many spin-offs sequels and other  versions  have also been broadcast  there have also been many translations  and retellings in english  most recently from valmiki sanskrit  telling  under the general editorship of american  scholars  robert and sally goldman but critics  of the brahmanic system of arnaz and  dharma have also used the story of rama  to prove a very different history for  example  there is a substantial jati or birth  group that proudly calls itself the  descendants  and followers of valmiki although elite  hindus used to class them as untouchable  before untouchability became illegal in  today's india  so various communities have challenged  their low status  in the brahmonic system by using the  prestige of  thoraminum  over the last few centuries many fiction  writers have also used  episodes or characters from the ramayana  to convey a variety of messages  that clash with those of valmiki's  tellings  feminist short story authors and  playwrights celebrate sita  often portraying her as a strong  independent woman  wronged by an insensitive husband  some social reformers have critiqued  valmiki's  and tulsidas's orthodox tellings of the  life of rama  claiming those elite authors justify his  abuses  of his lower class and lower varna  subjects  most prominently a culturalist  nationalist movement  in the tamil speaking region of south  india called the dravidian organization  or dk movement  started in the 1930s to attack brahman  domination  although brahmanas were a relatively  small minority  in the tamil region they controlled much  of the political  and economic power there under the  charismatic  eevee ramasamy known as evr  this dravidian movement pronounced the  ramayana to be the history  of an unjust invasion by north indians  according to evr valmiki describes the  inhabitants of south india as merely  monkeys and bears  and the inhabitants of sri lanka as  demons  evr argued in print and dramatized in  the streets of that  tamil speakers and other southerners  must discredit  the brahmanic religious system that  treated them as  sub-human until they did that they would  remain  mentally under the north indian  brahmanic cultural hegemony or  domination  therefore evr mounted massive public  processions  in which images of rama were beaten with  defiling shoes  and pictures of him burnt to show the  contempt the tamils  should have for rama and brahmanism  out of this tamil cultural movement came  the political parties that have  dominated the government  in tamilnadu state for many decades  the narrative and sacred power of the  ramayana has meant that it spread beyond  india  to southeast asia especially in  indonesia cambodia  thailand and burma now myanmar  in indonesia an account of rama in old  javanese dates from about 870.  one of java's most popular shadow puppet  plays  also features the story of rama  cambodia's famous 12th century angkor  wat temple  has many stone carved panels and statues  illustrating the ramayana then around  1350  the king of thailand renamed his capital  ayutya  after rama's capital ayoja  from the 18th century a burmese version  of the ramayana became the burmese  national epic  so we'll as we'll see for buddhism and  islam hindu cultural influences spread  from india far to the east but as rama's  story moved  local cultures adapted it to their own  cultural values and artistic forms  although their attitudes toward the  story of rama their methodologies and  their conclusions often differ  both emic and ethic readers can learn  much  about the ancient indian history from  ramayana  in the next couple of lectures we'll  discuss in similar ways the other  major indian epic the mahabharata and  its account of ancient  indian history  [Music]  you
 

